Overview
This project builds an end-to-end machine learning pipeline to predict salaries based on job-related features. It handles numerical and categorical data, performs preprocessing, exploratory data analysis (EDA), and compares multiple regression models, including Linear Regression, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and XGBoost.
The pipeline is ready for deployment and can easily be integrated with web apps (e.g., Streamlit).

Technologies Used
Python 3
Pandas, NumPy
scikit-learn
XGBoost
Matplotlib, Seaborn
Joblib (model saving)

Dataset
CSV dataset with the following features:
Job Title (categorical)
Education (categorical)
Age (numerical)
YearsExperience (numerical)
Salary (target)
Dataset must be cleaned of missing target values (Salary) for training.

Project Steps
1.Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)
Visualized salary distribution
Examined correlations between numeric features (Age, YearsExperience) and Salary
Analyzed Salary vs Education and top 10 Job Titles using boxplots

2.Preprocessing
Filled missing numeric values with median
Filled missing categorical values with "Unknown"
Encoded categorical variables using OneHotEncoder
Scaled numerical features using StandardScaler

3.Pipeline & Model Training
Combined preprocessing with model training using Pipeline
Compared models:
Linear Regression
Random Forest Regressor
Gradient Boosting Regressor
XGBoost Regressor

Evaluated models using:
MAE (Mean Absolute Error)
RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error)
R² Score

4.Feature Importance
For tree-based models (Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost), top features influencing salary were extracted to gain insights.

